From: AR0234 port <local>
Subject: [PATCH 0011] vin: CSI bandwidth is always 0 (integer truncation)

vin_status_dump() computed

	vinc->bandwidth = vinc->vin_status.buf_size *
			  (1000/vinc->vin_status.frame_internal/1000);

frame_internal is the measured frame interval in microseconds, so
1000/frame_internal is 0 for every frame rate below 1000 fps and the whole
product collapses to 0.  "CSI Bandwidth" therefore always read 0M in
/sys/kernel/debug/mpp/vi and the per-vind total followed it.

Compute bytes/second directly and guard the (0 before the first frame)
division.  At 1920x1200 NV12/120 fps this now reports ~414 MB/s, which is what
1920*1200*1.5*120 gives by hand.

Signed-off-by: AR0234 port <local>
---

--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_core.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_core.c
@@ -958,7 +958,18 @@
 			   vinc->vin_status.frame_internal/1000,
 			   vinc->vin_status.max_internal/1000,
 			   vinc->vin_status.min_internal/1000,
-			   vinc->bandwidth = vinc->vin_status.buf_size * (1000/vinc->vin_status.frame_internal/1000));
+			   /*
+			    * frame_internal is in microseconds, so the old
+			    *   buf_size * (1000/frame_internal/1000)
+			    * was integer-truncated to 0 for every frame rate below
+			    * 1000 fps and "CSI Bandwidth" always read 0.  Compute in
+			    * bytes/second, which is also what the totals below divide
+			    * by 1 MiB.  frame_internal is 0 before the first frame is
+			    * measured, so guard the division.
+			    */
+			   vinc->bandwidth = vinc->vin_status.frame_internal ?
+				   (unsigned int)(((unsigned long long)vinc->vin_status.buf_size * 1000000ULL) /
+						  vinc->vin_status.frame_internal) : 0);
 	vind->csi_bd_tatol += vinc->bandwidth;
 	count += scnprintf(buf + count, size - count, "*****************************************************\n");
 
